From 6ec69f2d1c0502a9ffb75d60351b5f9a6b4f057e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alessandro Fontana <alessandro.fontana@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 22:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] extcon: sm5502: re-run detection for a cable present at boot

The chip decides what a cable is when it is plugged in, writes that into
DEV_TYPE1, and never measures again.  A cable that was already connected
when the system started was therefore classified by the bootloader,
before the USB host had enumerated anything, and a charging downstream
port comes out recorded as a plain SDP one.

On a Galaxy Tab A (SM-T580) this means booting with the cable in leaves
DEV_TYPE1 at 0x04 indefinitely.  The extcon device reports SDP, a charger
driver consuming it settles on the 500 mA limit, and with the screen on
that does not cover the load: the battery drains on a connected cable
until somebody unplugs and replugs it by hand.  Unplugging is what makes
the chip measure again, and it then reports 0x20 - a CDP - and the limit
rises to 1.5 A.

The delayed work that already exists for exactly this situation runs
seventeen seconds after probe, long after enumeration, but it only reads
a decision that was made much earlier.  Reset the chip there so it
measures the cable afresh, then restore the register configuration,
which the reset clears along with the interrupt masks.  The attach
interrupt that follows the reset carries the new classification through
the normal path.

The vendor driver does the same thing from its own boot-time work, on a
shorter timer.
---
 drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c b/drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c
index c2a0d94dbc..d6088abea4 100644
--- a/drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c
+++ b/drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c
@@ -699,12 +699,28 @@ static irqreturn_t sm5502_muic_irq_handler(int irq, void *data)
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
 
+static void sm5502_init_dev_type(struct sm5502_muic_info *info);
+
 static void sm5502_muic_detect_cable_wq(struct work_struct *work)
 {
 	struct sm5502_muic_info *info = container_of(to_delayed_work(work),
 				struct sm5502_muic_info, wq_detcable);
 	int ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * The chip classifies a cable once, when it is plugged in, and never
+	 * revisits that decision.  A cable already present at boot was
+	 * therefore measured by the bootloader, under whatever configuration
+	 * it had left behind, and a charging downstream port can end up
+	 * recorded as a plain SDP one.
+	 *
+	 * Reset the chip so it measures again, now that the host has had time
+	 * to enumerate, and put the configuration back afterwards: the reset
+	 * clears the interrupt masks along with everything else.
+	 */
+	regmap_write(info->regmap, SM5502_REG_RESET, SM5502_REG_RESET_MASK);
+	sm5502_init_dev_type(info);
+
 	/* Notify the state of connector cable or not  */
 	ret = sm5502_muic_cable_handler(info, true);
 	if (ret < 0)
-- 
2.47.3

